// The online system on a workstation: the real control loop, and the modelled
// machine behind the real seam.
//
// What this exists for is configuration, not physics. The online composition
// next door -- the control process, the logger process, the process
// descriptions, the shared-memory namespace, the log writer's own open -- has no
// way to be exercised without a powered unit, so every one of those facts is
// first tried at a machine that can move. This module closes that gap: the same
// `robot::RobotBox`, unmodified, talking over the same loopback ports to the
// simulated plant the deterministic scenarios already prove, all of it under the
// same launcher. A run either produces a log that shows the wake gesture or it
// names the configuration that is wrong, on a workstation, for free.
//
// The plant is `sim::MotorSim` as it stands. It consumes and produces exactly
// the seven subjects the seam carries, and the seam's wire format is each schema's
// Tachyon bytes at their own size -- which is what a `udp_socket` declaration
// reads and writes. So the stand-in for `reachy-motord` here is not a program of
// its own: it is that cog, in a process of its own, with sockets where `SimBox`
// wires channel to channel. Nothing about the plant is re-implemented and
// nothing about it is configured differently -- `cogs/sim_params.textproto` is
// the file ten scenarios run on.
//
// The channels below are this module's own rather than `motion`'s, and that is
// forced: every channel has exactly one publisher, and in this system the
// control box's incoming sockets are the publishers of the `motion` channels the
// driver fills. The plant's ends therefore need channels of their own, one per
// subject, sized as their counterparts are. The seam between them is the
// sockets, which is the point -- a datagram really is encoded, sent, received
// and decoded here, exactly as it will be on a unit.
//
// `MotorSim` runs on `time_since_last_exec(20ms)`, which under the wall-clock
// runner is the wall clock rather than a simulated grid. None of the reasons the
// real driver cannot be a cog applies to it: it holds no serial port, blocks on
// nothing, and owns no I/O but its own channels.
//
// What a run of this proves is the composition and the configuration -- the
// process descriptions, the pinion namespace, the launcher config, the seam's
// wire contract, the writer's open, and the analyzer over what it wrote. What it
// cannot prove: serial timing, this device's kernel, aarch64, and anything at
// all about real servos. Those belong to the unit and to the bench's own
// self-tests.
//
// The foot of this file is the composition: the two boxes, the machine they run
// on, and the three processes that host them -- here rather than in the system
// module next door because the grammar admits a `system_target` only in a module
// that generates nothing, and a process needs a module that generates an
// executable.

#![generate(cpp, cpp_exe)]
#![cpp(namespace=brenn_reachy::robot_host)]

use [cpp, proto, proto_conv] cogs::config;
use cogs::robot;
use cogs::session_cmd;
use cogs::sim;
use cogs::sim_state;
use driver::goal;
use driver::health;
use driver::pose;
use @clockwork::clockwork::logging::log_writer_config;
use [cpp, proto, proto_conv] @clockwork::clockwork::logging::writers::channel_message_rates_config;
use @clockwork::clockwork::logging::writers::logger;
use [] @clockwork::clockwork::logging::writers::logger_channels;
use [cpp, proto, proto_conv] @clockwork::clockwork::logging::writers::logger_config;
use @clockwork::clockwork::logging::writers::logger_status;

// The goal stream as the plant sees it: what arrived on the goal port, before
// the gate decides which setpoint is due. Sized as `motion::DriverCmd` is, for
// the same reason -- a goal the plant never saw is a goal the machine was never
// asked for.
channel HostGoalChan
{
    message_type: Tachyon<goal::GoalSetpoint>;
    max_num_messages: 16;
}

// What the session asked the driver for, as it arrives on the session port.
// Sized as `motion::SessionCmdChan` is: each datagram is liveness as well as a
// request.
channel HostSessionCmdChan
{
    message_type: Tachyon<session_cmd::SessionCmd>;
    max_num_messages: 16;
}

// The plant's sample stream on its way out, and the plant's own read-back of it
// for the wire sequence number. Sized as `motion::DriverPose` is.
channel HostPoseChan
{
    message_type: Tachyon<pose::PoseSample>;
    max_num_messages: 16;
}

// The edges the plant reported, on their way out.
channel HostEvtChan
{
    message_type: Tachyon<health::DriverEvent>;
    max_num_messages: 16;
}

// How one out-of-band transaction turned out, on its way out.
channel HostAuxOutChan
{
    message_type: Tachyon<health::AuxOutcome>;
    max_num_messages: 8;
}

// What the plant's rotating read of the status registers saw, on its way out.
channel HostHealthChan
{
    message_type: Tachyon<health::HealthReport>;
    max_num_messages: 16;
}

// What the plant says about the whole run so far, on its way out. Small: every
// message is the whole account, so a reader that saw one has read the run and
// depth is slack for a busy consumer rather than a rate anything follows.
channel HostStatusChan
{
    message_type: Tachyon<health::DriverStatus>;
    max_num_messages: 8;
}

// The plant's injection channel, with nothing in it, ever.
//
// A scenario reaches the modelled world through this channel: jam a joint, write
// an error byte, drop a cycle's replies. An online run has no such hand -- what
// the plant does is whatever the control loop asks of it and nothing else -- so
// this channel exists only because the plant's view of it is not optional and
// every channel in a system must have a publisher. Its publisher is
// `HostSimCmdIn` below, a bound port nothing sends to, so the plant reads the
// empty view pinion gives a consumer of a channel nobody has published on. That
// is exactly the no-injections behaviour wanted here.
//
// A cog that publishes nothing would have served equally, and would have had to
// be C++: the Rust generator refuses a module that declares a `udp_socket`. A
// bound port that nothing addresses costs a file descriptor and says the same
// thing.
//
// Sized as the scenarios' own is, so the two cannot be read as different
// channels.
channel HostSimCmdChan
{
    message_type: Tachyon<sim_state::SimCmd>;
    max_num_messages: 16;
}

// The goal port, bound by the plant. This is the port `reachy-motord` binds on a
// unit, and this socket is the whole of what stands where it does.
//
// Loopback, and the trust that comes with it is the seam's own, stated once at
// `robot.clk`'s sockets and unchanged by anything here: nothing off this machine
// can reach these ports, any process on it can. On a workstation the sockets are
// also the reason two runs cannot share a host -- both would bind the same
// ports -- which is the same refusal the launcher makes first, by name.
udp_socket HostGoalIn
{
    address: 127.0.0.1;
    port: 7401;
    direction: incoming;
    message_type: Tachyon<goal::GoalSetpoint>;
}

// The session port, bound by the plant: keep the dead-man fed, take torque off,
// run this transaction.
udp_socket HostSessionIn
{
    address: 127.0.0.1;
    port: 7406;
    direction: incoming;
    message_type: Tachyon<session_cmd::SessionCmd>;
}

// The injection port: bound, listened on, and never sent to. It is the publisher
// `HostSimCmdChan` names above, where the reason that channel must have one is
// stated.
//
// It is deliberately not one of the seam's subjects: a datagram on this seam is
// identified by the port it arrived on, and this subject exists in no driver.
// Which is also why its number has to stay clear of every port
// `crates/reachy-motord/src/ports.rs` names: this composition puts the control
// box's incoming ports and the plant's on one loopback, so a number the seam
// uses is a number two processes here would bind.
udp_socket HostSimCmdIn
{
    address: 127.0.0.1;
    port: 7408;
    direction: incoming;
    message_type: Tachyon<sim_state::SimCmd>;
}

// The sample stream, leaving for the control process. `port` on an outgoing
// socket is the destination -- 7402 is the port the control box binds, not one
// this process holds.
udp_socket HostPoseOut
{
    address: 127.0.0.1;
    port: 7402;
    direction: outgoing;
    message_type: Tachyon<pose::PoseSample>;
}

// The edges the plant reported, leaving.
udp_socket HostEvtOut
{
    address: 127.0.0.1;
    port: 7403;
    direction: outgoing;
    message_type: Tachyon<health::DriverEvent>;
}

// How one out-of-band transaction turned out, leaving.
udp_socket HostAuxOutOut
{
    address: 127.0.0.1;
    port: 7404;
    direction: outgoing;
    message_type: Tachyon<health::AuxOutcome>;
}

// What the rotating read saw, leaving.
udp_socket HostHealthOut
{
    address: 127.0.0.1;
    port: 7405;
    direction: outgoing;
    message_type: Tachyon<health::HealthReport>;
}

// What the plant says about its whole run, leaving.
udp_socket HostStatusOut
{
    address: 127.0.0.1;
    port: 7407;
    direction: outgoing;
    message_type: Tachyon<health::DriverStatus>;
}

// The driver's half, hosted: the modelled plant with the seam's own sockets on
// every edge it has.
//
// `SimBox` is the wiring template and this is that wiring with a socket and a
// channel of its own inserted in each edge. The plant's own loop -- reading back
// the last sample it published for the wire sequence number -- is closed here
// too, off this box's pose channel rather than off `motion::DriverPose`, because
// in this system that channel belongs to the control process.
box SimDriver
{
    new plant: sim::MotorSim;

    // The plant's own slot, written by nothing else. No `init=`, so it is
    // default-constructed into a buffer the compiler synthesizes.
    new plant_state: State(representation=Tachyon<sim_state::SimState>);
    connect plant_state to plant.sim;

    // The cycle, the dead-man and the modelled servos' speed: the scenarios'
    // own file, unchanged, so the plant here is the plant they prove.
    new plant_params: SerializedDataFile(
        representation=Protobuf<config::SimParams>,
        path="cogs/sim_params.textproto");
    connect plant_params to plant.params;

    // What the control process asked for, off the wire.
    new goal_in: HostGoalIn;
    connect goal_in to HostGoalChan;
    connect HostGoalChan to plant.goals;

    new session_in: HostSessionIn;
    connect session_in to HostSessionCmdChan;
    connect HostSessionCmdChan to plant.session_cmds;

    // The hand that is not here.
    new sim_cmd_in: HostSimCmdIn;
    connect sim_cmd_in to HostSimCmdChan;
    connect HostSimCmdChan to plant.cmds;

    // The sample stream: out on the wire, and back into the plant.
    connect plant.pose to HostPoseChan;
    connect HostPoseChan to plant.own_pose;
    new pose_out: HostPoseOut;
    connect HostPoseChan to pose_out;

    connect plant.evt to HostEvtChan;
    new evt_out: HostEvtOut;
    connect HostEvtChan to evt_out;

    connect plant.aux_out to HostAuxOutChan;
    new aux_out: HostAuxOutOut;
    connect HostAuxOutChan to aux_out;

    connect plant.health_out to HostHealthChan;
    new health_out: HostHealthOut;
    connect HostHealthChan to health_out;

    connect plant.status to HostStatusChan;
    new status_out: HostStatusOut;
    connect HostStatusChan to status_out;
}

// What writes a host run down: the framework's two writer cogs, in a process of
// their own, exactly as the device composition has them.
//
// A duplicate of `robot::RobotLogger` and not a parameterization of it, because
// the two files it binds are bound by literal path: the writer's channel set
// comes out of *this* system's target, and the log root is a directory on this
// workstation. A box that took either as a parameter would be a box that can be
// pointed at a device path from a host run, which is the class of configuration
// lie this whole module exists to catch.
//
// Logging is observation only here as well: no channel leaves this box, so a
// logger that died costs the run its records and nothing else.
box RobotLoggerHost
{
    new init_cog: logger::LoggerInitCog;
    new status_publisher_cog: logger::LoggerStatusPublisherCog;

    // The writer's arena, sized as the device's is: one second of the configured
    // write rate plus the 8 MiB schema reserve, in 256 KiB buffers, and headroom
    // for the per-channel bookkeeping.
    new memory: HeapMemory(max_size=33'554'432);

    // Which channels are written, generated from this system's own logging
    // policies. The same set the device system logs, so one analyzer reads
    // either log.
    new logged_channels: SerializedDataFile(
        representation=Tachyon<log_writer_config::LogWriterConfig<>>,
        path="cogs/system_host.motion_host.HostCpu_event_logger_config.tachyon");
    connect logged_channels to init_cog.log_writer_config;

    // Where the records go and where the channels are read from. A host path,
    // and the same flagless pinion defaults every process in every one of these
    // systems runs on.
    new logger_params: SerializedDataFile(
        representation=Protobuf<logger_config::LoggerConfig>,
        path="cogs/host_logger.textproto");
    connect logger_params to init_cog.logger_config;

    // What counts as a channel gone quiet, for the status message only: the
    // device's own file, because the rates are the system's and not the host's.
    new rate_floors: SerializedDataFile(
        representation=Protobuf<channel_message_rates_config::ChannelMessageRatesConfig>,
        path="cogs/robot_logger_rates.textproto");
    connect rate_floors to init_cog.channel_rates_config;

    // The writer itself, built on the first execution and kept from then on. It
    // holds a file descriptor and an io_uring across executions, which is why it
    // is an extern type in a state slot rather than anything schema-resident.
    new state: State(
        representation=logger::LoggerCogState,
        memory_resource=memory,
        init=init_cog.logger_state);
    connect state to status_publisher_cog.logger_state;

    // What the logger says about itself, logged like anything else.
    connect init_cog.logger_config_out to logger_channels::LoggerConfigChannel;
    connect status_publisher_cog.logger_status to logger_channels::LoggerStatusChannel;
    connect status_publisher_cog.channel_rates to logger_channels::LoggerMessageRatesChannel;
}

// The machine this runs on: a workstation.
//
// One domain for all three processes, because the seam between them is sockets
// and their channels are shared memory -- the same arrangement the unit has, with
// the driver's process being a Clockwork one here. The launcher config this
// domain renders names all three, each with nothing but its process description
// as arguments, so every process runs on the compiled-in pinion defaults. There
// is no `simplelaunch_src` to merge: on a unit the driver is an OS process the
// renderer knows nothing about, and here it is not.
cpu_domain HostCpu;

// Three processes on one workstation over one executable, wall-clock: the
// control loop, the plant it commands, and the logger that watches both.
// `robot_host_clk_exe` is the executable the compiler synthesizes for this file
// under `cpp_exe`, named for the file stem; each process is started from it with
// its own process description.
//
// `online_main`, the same as the device composition's, because this is the
// online path: a deterministic run over live sockets would have nothing to read.
//
// The control box is `robot::RobotBox` with nothing done to it. That is the
// whole value of the exercise -- the wake gesture, the session, the mover, the
// clips and the seam's schemas here are the ones a unit runs.
box System
{
    new box: robot::RobotBox;

    new proc: Process(executable=robot_host_clk_exe);

    apply HostProcess(process=proc) in box;
    apply HostCpuDomain(cpu_domain=HostCpu) to proc;

    new plant_box: SimDriver;

    new plant_proc: Process(executable=robot_host_clk_exe);

    apply HostProcess(process=plant_proc) in plant_box;
    apply HostCpuDomain(cpu_domain=HostCpu) to plant_proc;

    new logger_box: RobotLoggerHost;

    new logger_proc: Process(executable=robot_host_clk_exe);

    apply HostProcess(process=logger_proc) in logger_box;
    apply HostCpuDomain(cpu_domain=HostCpu) to logger_proc;
}
